A good proposal tells the client you understand their problem, what they will get and what it costs. An AI assistant helps with the writing and structure, not with knowing your scope and prices. This guide gives a repeatable six-step method that fits in about 20 minutes once you have done it once or twice, and works with Claude, ChatGPT or Gemini. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer. Stackbrook did not test these tools, and has no affiliate relationship with any of them at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.

The time split below is a planning guide, not a measurement. The assistant does the drafting; the other steps are yours.

![Flow chart of a six-step proposal method with suggested minutes: gather inputs 4, brief the assistant 3, draft 2, challenge the numbers 5, tailor to the client 3, final human review 3, totalling 20 minutes](/images/site1/ai-assistant-client-proposal-in-20-minutes-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook as a suggested time budget; it is a planning guide, not measured data.*

## Step 1: Gather your inputs (4 minutes)

The quality of the draft depends on what you feed in. Before you open the assistant, collect these in a plain note:

- **The client's problem in their words.** Copy two or three sentences from their email or call notes, with names and sensitive details removed.
- **Your scope.** What is included, what is explicitly not, and how many revision rounds.
- **Your numbers.** Your own prices, hourly rate or day rate, the timeline, and payment terms. These come from you, never from the assistant.
- **Proof.** One or two past results you can honestly stand behind.
- **Constraints.** Deadline, how long the quote is valid, and any budget range the client mentioned.

## Step 2: Brief the assistant (3 minutes)

Tell the assistant its role, the audience, the structure you want and the rules. The rule that matters most is that it must not invent facts. A prompt you can copy and adapt:

```
You are helping me, a [your role, e.g. freelance web designer], write a
client proposal. Write in plain, friendly, professional English, no hype.

CLIENT (anonymised): [type of business, e.g. "a small dental clinic"]
THEIR PROBLEM: [2-3 sentences]
MY SCOPE (included): [bullets]
NOT INCLUDED: [bullets]
TIMELINE: [dates or weeks]
PRICE: [my figures exactly as written]
PAYMENT TERMS: [e.g. 50% upfront, 50% on delivery]
VALID FOR: [e.g. 30 days]
PROOF POINTS: [1-2 real results I can back up]

Structure: 1) Summary of your understanding of the problem, 2) Proposed
approach, 3) Deliverables, 4) Timeline, 5) Investment, 6) Next steps.
Keep it under 600 words.

RULES: Use only the facts and numbers I gave you. Do not invent results,
statistics, client names or guarantees. If something is missing, put it
in [SQUARE BRACKETS IN CAPITALS] instead of guessing. Do not change any price.
```

Placeholders in square brackets are your safety net. They make gaps visible instead of hiding them behind confident-sounding text.

If your assistant offers a feature for keeping reusable instructions or reference documents, such as Claude's Projects, you can store your standard structure and tone there. Anthropic's support page says Projects are available on all Claude plans including free (free accounts can create up to five), each with its own knowledge base and custom instructions, and that sharing with teammates is limited to Team and Enterprise plans. Check the equivalent features and your own plan for ChatGPT and Gemini before relying on them. The method works in a plain chat window too.

## Step 3: Let it draft (2 minutes)

Send the prompt and read the first draft once, quickly, without editing line by line. You are looking for structure problems: a missing section, a salesy tone, or something promised that you did not say. If the structure is off, reply with one specific correction, such as "shorten the summary to three sentences", rather than rewriting the whole prompt.

## Step 4: Challenge and fact-check the numbers (5 minutes)

This is the longest step on purpose. Assistants can produce fluent text that contains changed figures, invented claims or promises you never made. Go through the draft with your input note beside it and check:

1. **Every price, date and quantity** matches your note exactly, including totals. Add up line items yourself.
2. **Every claim about results** is something you can prove. Delete any statistic you did not provide.
3. **Scope matches.** Nothing extra has crept into "deliverables" that you would then owe for free.
4. **Guarantees and legal-sounding wording.** Remove anything that reads like a promise or contract term you did not intend.

Then ask the assistant to attack its own work: "List every number, date and factual claim in this proposal and tell me which ones came from my brief and which you added." Anything in the second group is a candidate for deletion. Treat that as a prompt for your own check, not proof the draft is clean.

## Step 5: Tailor it to the client (3 minutes)

A proposal that could be sent to anyone gets treated like one. Add one or two specifics only you would know, such as something the client said in the call or their deadline pressure. Match the length and formality to how they write to you. If they are time-poor, put the summary and the price in the first half page.

## Step 6: Final human review (3 minutes)

Read the whole document top to bottom, slowly, or out loud if it is short. Check names, company spelling, the date, the validity period and your contact details. Ask yourself whether you would be comfortable if the client held you to every sentence, because they may. Then send it as your own work, in your own voice.

## What never to paste in

Treat a consumer chat window like a place you would not leave a client's file. Do not paste:

- Client contracts, NDAs, internal documents or anything marked confidential.
- Personal data such as client staff names, emails, phone numbers or ID details.
- Passwords, API keys, bank details or invoices with account numbers.

Anonymise instead: "a regional logistics company with about 40 staff" is enough for a draft. If you need to work with genuinely confidential material, read the vendor's terms for your specific plan first, and check whether your client's contract allows using AI tools at all.

## Check your assistant's data settings first

The controls differ, so look at yours before you paste anything. These are the vendors' own pages, checked on 12 November 2026:

- **Claude.** Anthropic's privacy center says that for its consumer plans, chats and coding sessions can be used to improve Claude if you choose to allow it, through a Model Improvement setting in Privacy Settings. It also says Incognito chats are not used to improve Claude, and that conversations flagged for safety review and feedback you submit can be handled differently. Its page says a thumbs up or down stores the related conversation for up to 5 years. Commercial products are covered in a separate article.
- **ChatGPT.** OpenAI's Data Controls page says that while the "Improve the model for everyone" setting is disabled, new conversations (not earlier ones) will not be used to train its models, and that Temporary Chats are not used for training and are removed within 30 days.
- **Gemini.** Google's privacy hub says that with Keep Activity on, your activity may be used to improve its services including training generative AI models, and that human reviewers read a sample of chats. With Keep Activity on, it advises not to enter confidential information you would not want a reviewer to see. With Keep Activity off, it says future chats are not used for AI training unless you send feedback, but are still kept for 72 hours.

Settings, defaults and plan terms change, and work or business accounts can follow different rules from personal ones. Open the linked pages and your account's settings today rather than relying on this summary.
